@page "/ai/scenarios/performance"
@inherits AppComponentBase

<PageOutlet Url="ai/scenarios/performance"
            Title="Long AI conversations"
            Keywords="Blazor chat performance, virtualized chat, long conversation rendering, BitAiMessageList virtualization, render coalescing"
            Description="Five hundred AI messages with code, tables, tool calls and citations, rendered through a virtualized list that follows new content only while the reader is at the bottom - bit BlazorUI." />

<DocsArticle Eyebrow="AI scenarios"
             Title="Long conversations"
             Lead="Five hundred messages with code, tables, tool calls, and citations, rendered through a virtualized list."
             GitHubUrl="AI/Scenarios/Performance/AiPerformanceScenarioPage.razor">

    <Badges>
        <BitTag Size="BitSize.Small" Variant="BitVariant.Outline" Color="BitColor.SecondaryForeground" Text="Bit.BlazorUI.AI" />
        <BitTag Size="BitSize.Small" Variant="BitVariant.Outline" Color="BitColor.SecondaryForeground" Text="Scripted transport" />
    </Badges>

    <ChildContent>
        <DocSection Id="demo" Title="Live demo"
                    Description="Scroll to the top and back, then send something: new content follows the view only while you are already at the bottom.">
            <DemoExample Title="Five hundred messages" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example1CsharpCode" CodeFiles="@example1CodeFiles" Id="example1">
                <AiScenarioFrame>
                    <BitAiChat Session="_session" UiOptions="_options" />
                </AiScenarioFrame>
            </DemoExample>
        </DocSection>

        <DocSection Id="how-it-works" Title="How it works">
            <p>
                Up to a threshold every message is rendered, which is both simpler and faster. Past it
                (<code>BitAiUiOptions.VirtualizeThreshold</code>, sixty messages here) only the newest sixty are: scroll up
                and the older ones come in thirty at a time above what you are reading, which stays where it is, and once you
                are back at the newest message they are let go of again. Nothing is estimated - every message drawn is drawn
                at its own height - so the content never jumps and the view never goes blank. The conversation is
                deliberately varied - a thousand identical paragraphs would prove nothing; the hard case is mixed content
                with wildly different heights.
            </p>
            <p>
                The five hundred messages are built in code (<code>AiDemoConversations.Long</code>) - a
                <code>BitAiConversation</code> filled with <code>AddMessage</code>, each answer a
                <code>BitAiMessage</code> of parts: text, Markdown, a tool call with its result, a table with a citation - and
                handed to the session factory (<code>SessionFactory.Create(conversation)</code>), so new messages go to the
                transport registered for the site, which echoes them. A conversation restored from storage is handed over
                the same way (see <BitLink Href="/ai/scenarios/conversations">Conversations</BitLink>).
            </p>
            <p>Where the render budget goes:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    A part subscribes to itself, so a token re-renders the part it went into - not its message, nor the
                    transcript. The chat around it renders as a turn starts and as it ends.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Each of those views renders at most once per @(_options.RenderInterval.TotalMilliseconds)&nbsp;ms
                    (<code>BitAiUiOptions.RenderInterval</code>), however many tokens arrive in that time - coalesced by a
                    <code>BitAiRenderScheduler</code>, which an app's own component listening to a stream can use too (see
                    <BitLink Href="/ai/scenarios/streaming">Streaming</BitLink>).
                </li>
                <li>
                    Bursts of text are evened out before they reach the conversation: the session paces the deltas
                    (<code>BitAiChatSessionOptions.Pacing</code>), so a paragraph arriving at once is drawn over a few
                    frames rather than in one.
                </li>
                <li>Text accumulates in a builder, so appending stays linear.</li>
                <li>Markdown before the last block boundary is parsed once and reused.</li>
            </ul>
        </DocSection>

        <DocSection Id="components" Title="Components used">
            <ul>
                <li><BitLink Href="/components/ai/messagelist"><code>BitAiMessageList</code></BitLink> - the transcript, drawing only its newest messages past the threshold, with auto-scroll that follows only a reader at the newest message.</li>
                <li><BitLink Href="/components/ai/scrolltolatest"><code>BitAiScrollToLatest</code></BitLink> - the way back down once the reader has scrolled away.</li>
                <li><BitLink Href="/components/ai/messagepartview"><code>BitAiMessagePartView</code></BitLink> - one part, re-rendered only when it changes.</li>
            </ul>
        </DocSection>
    </ChildContent>

    <FooterTemplate>
        <DocPager Pages="AiScenarioNav.Items" AriaLabel="AI scenarios" />
    </FooterTemplate>
</DocsArticle>
